</span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Reinforcement learning agent development traditionally requires extensive expertise and lengthy iterations, often resulting in high failure rates and limited accessibility. This paper introduces Agent²—a novel agent-generates-agent framework that achieves fully automated RL agent design through intelligent LLM-driven generation. The system autonomously transforms natural language task descriptions and environment code into comprehensive, high-performance reinforcement learning solutions without human intervention.</p>
    
<p id="abstract1.2" class="ltx_p">Agent² features a revolutionary dual-agent architecture. The Generator Agent serves as an autonomous AI designer that analyzes tasks and generates executable RL agents, while the Target Agent is the resulting automatically generated RL agent.
The framework decomposes RL development into two distinct stages: MDP modeling and algorithmic optimization, enabling more targeted and effective agent generation.
Built on the Model Context Protocol, Agent² provides a unified framework that standardizes intelligent agent creation across diverse environments and algorithms, while incorporating adaptive training management and intelligent feedback analysis for continuous improvement.</p>
    
<p id="abstract1.3" class="ltx_p">Extensive experiments on a wide range of benchmarks—including MuJoCo, MetaDrive, MPE, and SMAC—demonstrate that Agent² consistently outperforms manually designed solutions across all tasks, achieving up to 55% performance improvement and substantial gains on average.
By enabling truly end-to-end, closed-loop automation, this work establishes a new paradigm in which intelligent agents design and optimize other agents, marking a fundamental breakthrough for automated AI systems.</p>
  
</div>
<div id="id1" class="ltx_logical-block ltx_align_bottom">
    
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="p1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><sup id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_sup">1</sup>Qiyuan Lab, Beijing, China</p>
<p id="p1.2" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center">lijianmin@qiyuanlab.com</p>
</div>
  
</div>
<section id="Sx1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Introduction</h2>

<div id="Sx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Reinforcement learning (RL) has achieved remarkable success in diverse domains such as robotics, games, and autonomous systems.
However, the process of developing high-performing RL agents remains notoriously complex, requiring substantial domain expertise, intricate environment engineering, careful algorithm selection, and tedious trial-and-error tuning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li 2017</a>)</cite>. This manual, expertise-driven workflow has become a critical bottleneck to the practical adoption and democratization of RL, resulting in high entry barriers, limited scalability, and suboptimal research efficiency.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Recent advances in large language models (LLMs) have demonstrated unprecedented capabilities in autonomous reasoning and code synthesis <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Achiam et al. 2023</a>)</cite>. These advancements make it possible to automate not just component-level tasks, but the entire RL agent development pipeline without human intervention.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we present <span id="Sx1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Agent<sup id="Sx1.p3.1.1.1" class="ltx_sup">2</sup></span>, an agent-generates-agent framework that achieves end-to-end automation of RL agent design and optimization using LLMs. Agent<sup id="Sx1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_sup">2</sup> introduces a novel dual-agent architecture: the <em id="Sx1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Generator Agent</em> acts as an autonomous AI designer capable of analyzing and generating all components required for RL agent; the resulting <em id="Sx1.p3.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Target Agent</em> is then constructed from these components and interacts with the environment for training and evaluation.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">A key innovation of Agent<sup id="Sx1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_sup">2</sup> is its intelligent two-stage decomposition of the RL development process. First, Agent<sup id="Sx1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_sup">2</sup> convert high-level task descriptions into formal MDP representations, capturing environment dynamics, rewards, and constraints. Next, Agent<sup id="Sx1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_sup">2</sup> automatically selects algorithms, designs neural architectures, and tunes hyperparameters, guided by LLM reasoning and empirical feedback. Integrated adaptive training and feedback analysis further enable robust error correction and iterative performance improvement through closed-loop interaction with the environment.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The remainder of this paper is organized as follows. We review related work, introduce our methodology, describe experimental details and results, and conclude with final remarks.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Related Work</h2>

<div id="Sx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Over the past few years, a wide range of general RL frameworks have been developed, such as RLlib <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liang et al. 2018</a>)</cite>, Stable Baselines3 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Raffin et al. 2021</a>)</cite>, Tianshou <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Weng et al. 2022</a>)</cite>, and Xuance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al. 2023</a>)</cite>.
These frameworks provide standardized implementations of algorithms, unified experiment management, and convenient interfaces for environment integration and distributed training.
While these systems have greatly facilitated RL research and application, developing RL agents still heavily depends on expert knowledge and manual intervention.
Currently, AutoML <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">He, Zhao, and Chu 2021</a>)</cite> has seen rapid development and become relatively mature, but automated reinforcement learning (AutoRL) remains more complex and is still in its early stages.
The ARLO framework <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mussi et al. 2023</a>)</cite> systematically modularizes the AutoRL pipeline and supports automated selection and optimization of most stage. However, its automation is limited, as key stages such as feature engineering and data preparation often depend on hand-crafted methods and expert knowledge.
Recently, LLMs have enabled new opportunities for RL automation by intelligently generating or optimizing key components of the RL pipeline <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cao et al. 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sun, Huang, and Pompili 2024</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For MDP modeling, some studies have explored automating the design of state representations, reward functions, and action spaces.
For state representation, YOLO-MARL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhuang et al. 2024</a>)</cite> and LERO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wei et al. 2025</a>)</cite> use LLMs to infer high-level or hidden context in multi-agent environment, helping to address limitations from partial observability. In single-agent scenarios, LESR <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. 2024</a>)</cite> and ExplorLLM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ma et al. 2024a</a>)</cite> use LLMs to autonomously generate or reformulate task-related state representations, improving learning efficiency and accelerating training.
Reward function design has also been extensively studied in both single-agent and multi-agent RL. In particular, single-agent studies have addressed the challenge of sparse rewards by developing automated reward shaping methods, especially for tasks that require complex robotic control <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ma et al. 2024b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Song et al. 2023</a>)</cite> or operate in high-level environments such as Minecraft <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. 2024</a>)</cite>.
In multi-agent settings, research mainly focuses on solving the credit assignment problem for effective reward distribution <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nagpal et al. 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lin et al. 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">He et al. 2025</a>)</cite>.
For action spaces, recent methods use LLM-generated action masking or suboptimal policies to dynamically constrain and guide RL agents, incorporating user preferences and improving sample efficiency and policy adaptability <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al. 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Karine and Marlin 2025</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Beyond these components, the design of neural network architectures and the optimization of hyperparameters also play a crucial role in RL performance. Recent work has thus explored automating both aspects to further reduce manual effort.
LLMatic <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nasir et al. 2024</a>)</cite> combines LLM code generation with quality diversity optimization to discover diverse and effective architectures, while EvoPrompting <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen, Dohan, and So 2023</a>)</cite> uses LMs as adaptive operators in evolutionary NAS.
In addition to network architecture design, recent studies have also leveraged LLMs to automate hyperparameter optimization in RL. Some studies use LLMs to suggest and iteratively refine hyperparameter configurations based on dataset and model descriptions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu, Gao, and Li 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al. 2023</a>)</cite>. Additionally, SLLMBO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mahammadli and Ertekin 2024</a>)</cite> introduces a sequential LLM-based framework that adapts the search space dynamically and enhances optimization robustness. These methods highlight the potential of LLMs to design the network architecture and optimize the hyperparameter of RL algorithms.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">However, most existing AutoRL approaches automate only a single stage of the RL pipeline, rather than enabling fully end-to-end, closed-loop agent generation.
As a result, substantial expert intervention is still required to bridge the gap between task specification and practical agent deployment.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Methods</h2>

<div id="Sx3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section provides an overview of the Agent<sup id="Sx3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_sup">2</sup> methodology, highlighting the automated workflow for RL agent generation. The overall process is illustrated in Figure <a href="#Sx3.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ Methods ‣ Agent²: An Agent-Generates-Agent Framework for Reinforcement Learning Automation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. This section focuses on the Generator Agent, which serves as the core driver of automation in the Agent<sup id="Sx3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_sup">2</sup> framework. The Generator Agent is responsible for analyzing tasks, structuring RL knowledge, and autonomously generating all components required for agent construction.</p>
</div>
<figure id="Sx3.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.13368v1/framework.png" id="Sx3.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:593/314;" width="593" height="314" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>Agent<sup id="Sx3.F1.2" class="ltx_sup">2</sup> processes natural language task descriptions, environment code, and constraints as input. Through problem analysis, it decomposes the task and assesses its complexity before invoking Model Context Protocol Services, which leverage LLMs for knowledge integration, natural language understanding, performance analysis, and code synthesis. The workflow then proceeds to MDP modeling—including state space, action space, and reward function design—followed by algorithmic optimization, where the agent autonomously selects algorithms, designs network architectures, and tunes hyperparameters. The generated components are assembled into the Target Agent, which is ready for training and evaluation. The entire process supports iterative refinement to enhance solution quality.</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="Sx3.SSx1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Task-to-MDP Mapping</h3>

<section id="Sx3.SSx1.SSSx1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Automatic Problem Analysis</h4>

<div id="Sx3.SSx1.SSSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx1.SSSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A key prerequisite for automated RL agent generation is the precise understanding and formalization of the target problem. To address this, Agent<sup id="Sx3.SSx1.SSSx1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_sup">2</sup> begins the RL pipeline with a dedicated problem analysis stage, systematically bridging the gap between high-level user intent and formal RL formulation.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx3.SSx1.SSSx1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx1.SSSx1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The Generator Agent leverages LLMs to process heterogeneous user input, typically including a natural language task description and, when available, environment code or specifications. The LLM integrates these inputs with its internal and, if configured, external knowledge bases, producing a structured representation of the RL problem. This analysis enables the system to: Identify learning objectives and reward structure from the task description; Infer state, action, and transition dynamics from the environment code; Extract explicit and implicit constraints, such as safety or operational limits; Anticipate challenges such as partial observability or delayed rewards.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx3.SSx1.SSSx1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx1.SSSx1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Formally, let the user provide three natural language inputs: task description<math id="Sx3.SSx1.SSSx1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\text{task}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mtext>task</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\text{task}}</annotation></semantics></math>, environment description or code<math id="Sx3.SSx1.SSSx1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\text{env}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mtext>env</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\text{env}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and additional context<math id="Sx3.SSx1.SSSx1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{c}</annotation></semantics></math>, such as constraints or prior knowledge. These are assembled into a prompt <math id="Sx3.SSx1.SSSx1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\text{analysis}}(T_{\text{task}},T_{\text{env}},T_{c})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>analysis</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mtext>task</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mtext>env</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\text{analysis}}(T_{\text{task}},T_{\text{env}},T_{c})</annotation></semantics></math>, which is processed by the LLM inference function <math id="Sx3.SSx1.SSSx1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\text{LLM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mtext>LLM</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\text{LLM}}</annotation></semantics></math>. The output <math id="Sx3.SSx1.SSSx1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\text{analysis}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mtext>analysis</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\text{analysis}}</annotation></semantics></math> is:</p>
<table id="Sx3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Sx3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\text{analysis}}=F_{\text{LLM}}(P_{\text{analysis}}(T_{\text{task}},T_{\text{env}},T_{c}))" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mtext>analysis</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mtext>LLM</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>analysis</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mtext>task</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mtext>env</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\text{analysis}}=F_{\text{LLM}}(P_{\text{analysis}}(T_{\text{task}},T_{\text{env}},T_{c}))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Sx3.SSx1.SSSx1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Here, <math id="Sx3.SSx1.SSSx1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\text{analysis}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mtext>analysis</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\text{analysis}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a structured analysis including formalized objectives, constraints, environment characteristics, and domain-specific challenges. This serves as the foundation for downstream MDP modeling and algorithm optimization, ensuring that all subsequent processes are both principled and context-aware.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx3.SSx1.SSSx2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">MDP Modeling</h4>

<div id="Sx3.SSx1.SSSx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx1.SSSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The MDP modeling module automates the refinement of initial, suboptimal RL environments into effective MDPs for reinforcement learning. In practice, available simulators or environments may only provide a coarse or incomplete MDP specification, requiring expert effort to redesign the observation space, action space, or reward function. This module leverages LLMs to analyze and optimize these core components, minimizing manual intervention. While LLMs are capable of constructing MDPs from scratch, our approach focuses on the more practical and challenging scenario of automatically optimizing an existing but imperfect environment.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx3.SSx1.SSSx2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx1.SSSx2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Formally, an MDP is defined as <math id="Sx3.SSx1.SSSx2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}=(\mathcal{S},\mathcal{A},\mathcal{P},\mathcal{R},\gamma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}=(\mathcal{S},\mathcal{A},\mathcal{P},\mathcal{R},\gamma)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="Sx3.SSx1.SSSx2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> is the state (observation) space, <math id="Sx3.SSx1.SSSx2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math> the action space, <math id="Sx3.SSx1.SSSx2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math> the transition probability, <math id="Sx3.SSx1.SSSx2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}</annotation></semantics></math> the reward function, and <math id="Sx3.SSx1.SSSx2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> the discount factor. Automated MDP modeling focuses on optimizing <math id="Sx3.SSx1.SSSx2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Sx3.SSx1.SSSx2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="Sx3.SSx1.SSSx2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}</annotation></semantics></math>, as these are central to agent-environment interaction.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx3.SSx1.SSSx2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx1.SSSx2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The LLM examines environment code and task objectives to identify and refine relevant state variables, possibly applying feature selection, dimensionality reduction, or combining observations to improve learning efficiency. The refined observation space is expressed as <math id="Sx3.SSx1.SSSx2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s^{\prime}=f_{\text{obs}}(s)\in\mathcal{S}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>obs</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s^{\prime}=f_{\text{obs}}(s)\in\mathcal{S}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="Sx3.SSx1.SSSx2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\text{obs}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>obs</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\text{obs}}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the learned or synthesized transformation function.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx3.SSx1.SSSx2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx1.SSSx2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Similarly, the action space is optimized to balance expressiveness and simplicity, with the LLM recommending discretization, merging, splitting, or reparameterization as needed: <math id="Sx3.SSx1.SSSx2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a^{\prime}=f_{\text{act}}(a)\in\mathcal{A}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>act</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a^{\prime}=f_{\text{act}}(a)\in\mathcal{A}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="Sx3.SSx1.SSSx2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\text{act}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>act</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\text{act}}</annotation></semantics></math> constructed through automatic analysis and synthesis.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx3.SSx1.SSSx2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx1.SSSx2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">For reward design, the LLM constructs functions that accurately reflect task objectives and promote effective learning, addressing issues such as sparsity or bias. The reward at time <math id="Sx3.SSx1.SSSx2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="Sx3.SSx1.SSSx2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{t}=f_{\text{rew}}(s_{t},a_{t},s_{t+1},\text{info})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>rew</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mtext>info</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{t}=f_{\text{rew}}(s_{t},a_{t},s_{t+1},\text{info})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="Sx3.SSx1.SSSx2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\text{rew}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>rew</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\text{rew}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the synthesized reward function.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx3.SSx1.SSSx2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx1.SSSx2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Each component is encapsulated as a wrapper function, enabling seamless integration and algorithmic verification within the RL pipeline.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx3.SSx1.SSSx3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Adaptive Verification and Refinement</h4>

<div id="Sx3.SSx1.SSSx3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx1.SSSx3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Automatically synthesized MDP components may have executability issues or lead to suboptimal learning due to ambiguities in code generation. To address this, we introduce an adaptive verification and refinement framework that integrates generated components into the RL pipeline and iteratively improves them via automated validation and feedback.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx3.SSx1.SSSx3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx1.SSSx3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This module aims to ensure that all synthesized components are executable, and optimized for agent learning. Each component—observation, action, and reward functions (<math id="Sx3.SSx1.SSSx3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f=\{f_{\text{obs}},f_{\text{act}},f_{\text{rew}}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>obs</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>act</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>rew</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f=\{f_{\text{obs}},f_{\text{act}},f_{\text{rew}}\}</annotation></semantics></math>)—is first integrated through standardized interfaces and checked by a verification operator <math id="Sx3.SSx1.SSSx3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math>. If verification fails, an error feedback <math id="Sx3.SSx1.SSSx3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> is generated and, together with the original RL analysis <math id="Sx3.SSx1.SSSx3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\text{analysis}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mtext>analysis</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\text{analysis}}</annotation></semantics></math> and component <math id="Sx3.SSx1.SSSx3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>, forms an adaptive prompt <math id="Sx3.SSx1.SSSx3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\text{error}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>error</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\text{error}}</annotation></semantics></math>. The LLM inference function <math id="Sx3.SSx1.SSSx3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\text{LLM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mtext>LLM</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\text{LLM}}</annotation></semantics></math> then synthesizes a revised component <math id="Sx3.SSx1.SSSx3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="Sx3.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="Sx3.E2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="Sx3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\text{If}\;V(f)=\text{False},\;\text{then}\;f^{*}\leftarrow F_{\text{LLM}}\big(P_{\text{error}}(e,L_{\text{analysis}},f)\big)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>If</mtext><mo lspace="0.280em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mtext>False</mtext></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext>then</mtext><mo lspace="0.280em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mtext>LLM</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>error</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mtext>analysis</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{If}\;V(f)=\text{False},\;\text{then}\;f^{*}\leftarrow F_{\text{LLM}}\big(P_{\text{error}}(e,L_{\text{analysis}},f)\big)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Sx3.SSx1.SSSx3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">This process repeats until all components pass verification.
Beyond static verification, the system continuously monitors the agent’s training dynamics and summarizes key performance indicators from TensorBoard data into a concise report, which serves as the performance feedback <math id="Sx3.SSx1.SSSx3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> for further optimization.
This feedback, together with <math id="Sx3.SSx1.SSSx3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\text{analysis}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mtext>analysis</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\text{analysis}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Sx3.SSx1.SSSx3.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>, and training history <math id="Sx3.SSx1.SSSx3.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math>, to construct a prompt <math id="Sx3.SSx1.SSSx3.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\text{perf}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>perf</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\text{perf}}</annotation></semantics></math>. The LLM then produces improved components:</p>
<table id="Sx3.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="Sx3.E3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="Sx3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle f^{*}\leftarrow F_{\text{LLM}}\big(P_{\text{perf}}(\epsilon,L_{\text{analysis}},f,\mathcal{H})\big)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mtext>LLM</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>perf</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mtext>analysis</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle f^{*}\leftarrow F_{\text{LLM}}\big(P_{\text{perf}}(\epsilon,L_{\text{analysis}},f,\mathcal{H})\big)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Sx3.SSx1.SSSx3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx1.SSSx3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">This closed-loop process ensures that MDP components are robust, reliable, and continually optimized, advancing the automation and practicality of LLM-based RL agent development.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx3.SSx1.SSSx3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx1.SSSx3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The procedure is summarized in Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ Adaptive Verification and Refinement ‣ Task-to-MDP Mapping ‣ Methods ‣ Agent²: An Agent-Generates-Agent Framework for Reinforcement Learning Automation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg1" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span> Task to MDP Mapping Pipeline</figcaption>
<div id="alg1.3" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg1.l0" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">0:</span>
                
               Task description <math id="alg1.l0.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\text{task}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mtext>task</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\text{task}}</annotation></semantics></math>, environment information <math id="alg1.l0.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\text{env}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mtext>env</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\text{env}}</annotation></semantics></math>, additional constraints <math id="alg1.l0.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{c}</annotation></semantics></math>, LLM inference function <math id="alg1.l0.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\text{LLM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mtext>LLM</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\text{LLM}}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l0a" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">0:</span>
                
               Verified and optimized MDP components <math id="alg1.l0a.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f=\{f_{\text{obs}},f_{\text{act}},f_{\text{rew}}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>obs</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>act</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>rew</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f=\{f_{\text{obs}},f_{\text{act}},f_{\text{rew}}\}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">1:</span>
                
               <span id="alg1.l1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">// Automatic Problem Analysis</span>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">2:</span>
                
               <math id="alg1.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\text{analysis}}=F_{\text{LLM}}(P_{\text{analysis}}(T_{\text{task}},T_{\text{env}},T_{c}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mtext>analysis</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mtext>LLM</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>analysis</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mtext>task</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mtext>env</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\text{analysis}}=F_{\text{LLM}}(P_{\text{analysis}}(T_{\text{task}},T_{\text{env}},T_{c}))</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">3:</span>
                
               <span id="alg1.l3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">// MDP Modeling</span>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">4:</span>
                
               <math id="alg1.l4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f=F_{\text{LLM}}(P_{\text{MDP}}(L_{\text{analysis}},T_{\text{env}},T_{c}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mtext>LLM</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>MDP</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mtext>analysis</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mtext>env</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f=F_{\text{LLM}}(P_{\text{MDP}}(L_{\text{analysis}},T_{\text{env}},T_{c}))</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">5:</span>
                
               <span id="alg1.l5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">// Adaptive Verification and Refinement</span>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">6:</span>
                
               Initialize best components <math id="alg1.l6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}\leftarrow f^{(0)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}\leftarrow f^{(0)}</annotation></semantics></math>, best score <math id="alg1.l6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{*}_{\text{hist}}\leftarrow S^{(0)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mtext>hist</mtext><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{*}_{\text{hist}}\leftarrow S^{(0)}</annotation></semantics></math>, training history <math id="alg1.l6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}\leftarrow\{\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}\leftarrow\{\}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l7" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">7:</span>
                
               <span id="alg1.l7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <math id="alg1.l7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=1</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="alg1.l7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>



</div>
<div id="alg1.l8" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">8:</span>
                
                Integrate <math id="alg1.l8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math> into RL pipeline

</div>
<div id="alg1.l9" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">9:</span>
                
                Apply verification operator <math id="alg1.l9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="alg1.l9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l10" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">10:</span>
                
                <span id="alg1.l10.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">if</span> verification fails (error <math id="alg1.l10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math>) <span id="alg1.l10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">then</span>



</div>
<div id="alg1.l11" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">11:</span>
                
                 <math id="alg1.l11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{(t+1)}\leftarrow F_{\text{LLM}}(P_{\text{error}}(e,L_{\text{analysis}},f^{(t)}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mtext>LLM</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>error</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mtext>analysis</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{(t+1)}\leftarrow F_{\text{LLM}}(P_{\text{error}}(e,L_{\text{analysis}},f^{(t)}))</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l12" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">12:</span>
                
                 <span id="alg1.l12.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">continue</span>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l13" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">13:</span>
                
                <span id="alg1.l13.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l13.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">if</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l14" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">14:</span>
                
                Train agent with <math id="alg1.l14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math>, collect TensorBoard metrics, update <math id="alg1.l14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l15" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">15:</span>
                
                Summarize key metrics into feedback <math id="alg1.l15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l16" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">16:</span>
                
                Obtain performance score <math id="alg1.l16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l17" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">17:</span>
                
                <span id="alg1.l17.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">if</span> <math id="alg1.l17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{(t)}&gt;S^{*}_{\text{hist}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>&gt;</mo><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mtext>hist</mtext><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{(t)}&gt;S^{*}_{\text{hist}}</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l17.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">then</span>



</div>
<div id="alg1.l18" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">18:</span>
                
                 <math id="alg1.l18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{*}_{\text{hist}}\leftarrow S^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mtext>hist</mtext><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{*}_{\text{hist}}\leftarrow S^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="alg1.l18.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}\leftarrow f^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}\leftarrow f^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l19" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">19:</span>
                
                <span id="alg1.l19.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l19.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">if</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l20" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">20:</span>
                
                <span id="alg1.l20.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">if</span> not converged <span id="alg1.l20.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">then</span>



</div>
<div id="alg1.l21" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">21:</span>
                
                 <math id="alg1.l21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{(t+1)}\leftarrow F_{\text{LLM}}(P_{\text{refine}}(\epsilon^{(t)},L_{\text{analysis}},f^{(t)},\mathcal{H}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mtext>LLM</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>refine</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mtext>analysis</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{(t+1)}\leftarrow F_{\text{LLM}}(P_{\text{refine}}(\epsilon^{(t)},L_{\text{analysis}},f^{(t)},\mathcal{H}))</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l22" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">22:</span>
                
                <span id="alg1.l22.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">else</span>


</div>
<div id="alg1.l23" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">23:</span>
                
                 break

</div>
<div id="alg1.l24" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">24:</span>
                
                <span id="alg1.l24.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l24.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">if</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l25" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">25:</span>
                
               <span id="alg1.l25.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l25.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l26" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">26:</span>
                
               <span id="alg1.l26.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">return</span>  Final verified and optimized MDP components <math id="alg1.l26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f=\{f_{\text{obs}},f_{\text{act}},f_{\text{rew}}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>obs</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>act</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>rew</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f=\{f_{\text{obs}},f_{\text{act}},f_{\text{rew}}\}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
</div>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="Sx3.SSx2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Algorithmic Optimization</h3>

<div id="Sx3.SSx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Upon completing the task-specific MDP design, Agent² automatically performs algorithm selection, network architecture design, training hyperparameter optimization, and integrated configuration with iterative refinement. The details of these four stages are presented in the following subsections.</p>
</div>
<section id="Sx3.SSx2.SSSx1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Algorithm Selection</h4>

<div id="Sx3.SSx2.SSSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx2.SSSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Algorithm selection aims to match the constructed MDP with an RL algorithm suitable for the environment’s characteristics and task requirements. For example, value-based methods like DQN are appropriate for discrete action spaces, whereas policy gradient methods such as PPO and SAC are better for continuous control. Choosing the right algorithm is crucial for effective and stable policy learning.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx3.SSx2.SSSx1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx2.SSSx1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Agent<sup id="Sx3.SSx2.SSSx1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_sup">2</sup> uses LLMs to adaptively select algorithms based on comprehensive information, including the structured RL analysis <math id="Sx3.SSx2.SSSx1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\text{analysis}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mtext>analysis</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\text{analysis}}</annotation></semantics></math>, environment code <math id="Sx3.SSx2.SSSx1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\text{env}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mtext>env</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\text{env}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and current MDP components <math id="Sx3.SSx2.SSSx1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f=\{f_{\text{obs}},f_{\text{act}},f_{\text{rew}}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>obs</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>act</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>rew</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f=\{f_{\text{obs}},f_{\text{act}},f_{\text{rew}}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Given a set of candidates <math id="Sx3.SSx2.SSSx1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, the LLM identifies the most suitable algorithm <math id="Sx3.SSx2.SSSx1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{*}\in G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{*}\in G</annotation></semantics></math> and provides a brief rationale <math id="Sx3.SSx2.SSSx1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\text{algo}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mtext>algo</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\text{algo}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Formally, the prompt is <math id="Sx3.SSx2.SSSx1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\text{alg}}(L_{\text{analysis}},f,T_{\text{env}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>alg</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mtext>analysis</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mtext>env</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\text{alg}}(L_{\text{analysis}},f,T_{\text{env}})</annotation></semantics></math>, and the LLM inference function performs:</p>
<table id="Sx3.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="Sx3.E4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="Sx3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle g^{*},\ L_{\text{algo}}=F_{\text{LLM}}\big(P_{\text{alg}}(G,f,L_{\text{analysis}},T_{\text{env}})\big)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mtext>algo</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mtext>LLM</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>alg</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mtext>analysis</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mtext>env</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle g^{*},\ L_{\text{algo}}=F_{\text{LLM}}\big(P_{\text{alg}}(G,f,L_{\text{analysis}},T_{\text{env}})\big)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx3.SSx2.SSSx2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Network Architecture Design</h4>

<div id="Sx3.SSx2.SSSx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx2.SSSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Effective reinforcement learning requires neural network architectures tailored to both the environment and the selected algorithm <math id="Sx3.SSx2.SSSx2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>. Agent<sup id="Sx3.SSx2.SSSx2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_sup">2</sup> integrates information from observation and action space descriptions, the algorithm, structured reference architectures (e.g., <math id="Sx3.SSx2.SSSx2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{\text{net}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>J</mi><mtext>net</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{\text{net}}</annotation></semantics></math> in JSON), and statistical summaries from training history (<math id="Sx3.SSx2.SSSx2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math>). Using these structured prompts, the LLM critiques and refines previous designs, leveraging both domain expertise and empirical results to suggest architectural improvements.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx3.SSx2.SSSx2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx2.SSSx2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Formally, the network design process is defined as:</p>
<table id="Sx3.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="Sx3.E5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="Sx3.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle N^{*},\ L_{\text{net}}=F_{\text{LLM}}\Big(P_{\text{net}}(g^{*},f_{\text{obs}},f_{\text{act}},J_{\text{net}},\mathcal{H})\Big)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mtext>net</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mtext>LLM</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>net</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>obs</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>act</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mtext>net</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle N^{*},\ L_{\text{net}}=F_{\text{LLM}}\Big(P_{\text{net}}(g^{*},f_{\text{obs}},f_{\text{act}},J_{\text{net}},\mathcal{H})\Big)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Sx3.SSx2.SSSx2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="Sx3.SSx2.SSSx2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>N</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> is the recommended architecture and <math id="Sx3.SSx2.SSSx2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\text{net}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mtext>net</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\text{net}}</annotation></semantics></math> explains the design. This automated approach enables Agent<sup id="Sx3.SSx2.SSSx2.p2.2.1" class="ltx_sup">2</sup> to generate adaptive network architectures for reliable RL training across diverse environments.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx3.SSx2.SSSx3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Hyperparameter Optimization</h4>

<div id="Sx3.SSx2.SSSx3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx2.SSSx3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Training performance in RL depends heavily on careful hyperparameter selection and adjustment, such as learning rate, discount factor, batch size, and regularization. Agent<sup id="Sx3.SSx2.SSSx3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_sup">2</sup> uses an LLM-driven process to systematically initialize and refine hyperparameters for the current environment, algorithm <math id="Sx3.SSx2.SSSx3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, and network architecture <math id="Sx3.SSx2.SSSx3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>N</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx3.SSx2.SSSx3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx2.SSSx3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The Generator Agent consolidates structured information from the MDP model (<math id="Sx3.SSx2.SSSx3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\text{obs}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>obs</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\text{obs}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Sx3.SSx2.SSSx3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\text{act}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>act</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\text{act}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Sx3.SSx2.SSSx3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\text{rew}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>rew</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\text{rew}}</annotation></semantics></math>), network <math id="Sx3.SSx2.SSSx3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>N</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, selected algorithm <math id="Sx3.SSx2.SSSx3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, reference configurations <math id="Sx3.SSx2.SSSx3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{\text{hp}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>J</mi><mtext>hp</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{\text{hp}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and training history <math id="Sx3.SSx2.SSSx3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math>. Prompt engineering guides the LLM to assess current settings, identify bottlenecks, and propose incremental, interpretable adjustments prioritized for training stability and performance.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx3.SSx2.SSSx3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx2.SSSx3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Formally, hyperparameter optimization is defined as:</p>
<table id="Sx3.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="Sx3.E6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="Sx3.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle Hp^{*},\ L_{\text{hp}}=F_{\text{LLM}}\Big(P_{\text{hp}}(g^{*},N^{*},f_{\text{obs}},f_{\text{act}},f_{\text{rew}},J_{\text{hp}},\mathcal{H})\Big)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mtext>hp</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mtext>LLM</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>hp</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>obs</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>act</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>rew</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mtext>hp</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle Hp^{*},\ L_{\text{hp}}=F_{\text{LLM}}\Big(P_{\text{hp}}(g^{*},N^{*},f_{\text{obs}},f_{\text{act}},f_{\text{rew}},J_{\text{hp}},\mathcal{H})\Big)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Sx3.SSx2.SSSx3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="Sx3.SSx2.SSSx3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Hp^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Hp^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> is the optimized hyperparameter set and <math id="Sx3.SSx2.SSSx3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\text{hp}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mtext>hp</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\text{hp}}</annotation></semantics></math> provides concise justifications for each change.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx3.SSx2.SSSx4" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Configuration Integration and Refinement</h4>

<div id="Sx3.SSx2.SSSx4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx2.SSSx4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">After algorithm selection, network architecture design, and hyperparameter optimization, Agent<sup id="Sx3.SSx2.SSSx4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_sup">2</sup> automatically integrates the selected algorithm <math id="Sx3.SSx2.SSSx4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, network <math id="Sx3.SSx2.SSSx4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>N</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, and optimized hyperparameters <math id="Sx3.SSx2.SSSx4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Hp^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Hp^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> into a unified configuration <math id="Sx3.SSx2.SSSx4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is exported in standardized YAML format for compatibility and reproducibility.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx3.SSx2.SSSx4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx2.SSSx4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Agent<sup id="Sx3.SSx2.SSSx4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_sup">2</sup> also supports adaptive refinement: after deployment, it continuously monitors training performance through TensorBoard data collection and processing. When needed, the LLM is leveraged to incrementally update <math id="Sx3.SSx2.SSSx4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math> based on observed results and historical data <math id="Sx3.SSx2.SSSx4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math>, forming a closed-loop process that continuously enhances agent performance and robustness.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx3.SSx2.SSSx4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx2.SSSx4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The complete algorithmic optimization pipeline is summarized in algorithm <a href="#alg2" title="Algorithm 2 ‣ Configuration Integration and Refinement ‣ Algorithmic Optimization ‣ Methods ‣ Agent²: An Agent-Generates-Agent Framework for Reinforcement Learning Automation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg2" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 2</span> </span> Algorithmic Optimization Pipeline</figcaption>
<div id="alg2.3" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg2.l0" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">0:</span>
                
               MDP components <math id="alg2.l0.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f=\{f_{\text{obs}},f_{\text{act}},f_{\text{rew}}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>obs</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>act</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>rew</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f=\{f_{\text{obs}},f_{\text{act}},f_{\text{rew}}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, problem analysis <math id="alg2.l0.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\text{analysis}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mtext>analysis</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\text{analysis}}</annotation></semantics></math>, environment code <math id="alg2.l0.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\text{env}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mtext>env</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\text{env}}</annotation></semantics></math>, LLM inference function <math id="alg2.l0.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\text{LLM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mtext>LLM</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\text{LLM}}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l0a" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">0:</span>
                
               Optimized RL configuration <math id="alg2.l0a.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">1:</span>
                
               <span id="alg2.l1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">// Algorithm Selection</span>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">2:</span>
                
               <math id="alg2.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{*},\ L_{\text{algo}}=F_{\text{LLM}}(P_{\text{alg}}(G,f,L_{\text{analysis}},T_{\text{env}}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mtext>algo</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mtext>LLM</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>alg</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mtext>analysis</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mtext>env</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{*},\ L_{\text{algo}}=F_{\text{LLM}}(P_{\text{alg}}(G,f,L_{\text{analysis}},T_{\text{env}}))</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">3:</span>
                
               <span id="alg2.l3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">// Network Architecture Design</span>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">4:</span>
                
               <math id="alg2.l4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N^{*},\ L_{\text{net}}=F_{\text{LLM}}(P_{\text{net}}(g^{*},f_{\text{obs}},f_{\text{act}},J_{\text{net}},\mathcal{H}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mtext>net</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mtext>LLM</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>net</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>obs</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>act</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mtext>net</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N^{*},\ L_{\text{net}}=F_{\text{LLM}}(P_{\text{net}}(g^{*},f_{\text{obs}},f_{\text{act}},J_{\text{net}},\mathcal{H}))</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">5:</span>
                
               <span id="alg2.l5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">// Hyperparameter Optimization</span>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">6:</span>
                
               <math id="alg2.l6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Hp^{*},\ L_{\text{hp}}=F_{\text{LLM}}(P_{\text{hp}}(g^{*},N^{*},f_{\text{obs}},f_{\text{act}},f_{\text{rew}},J_{\text{hp}},\mathcal{H}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mtext>hp</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mtext>LLM</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>hp</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>obs</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>act</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>rew</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mtext>hp</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Hp^{*},\ L_{\text{hp}}=F_{\text{LLM}}(P_{\text{hp}}(g^{*},N^{*},f_{\text{obs}},f_{\text{act}},f_{\text{rew}},J_{\text{hp}},\mathcal{H}))</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l7" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">7:</span>
                
               <span id="alg2.l7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">// Configuration Integration</span>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l8" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">8:</span>
                
               Merge <math id="alg2.l8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="alg2.l8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>N</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="alg2.l8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Hp^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Hp^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> into configuration <math id="alg2.l8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l9" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">9:</span>
                
               <span id="alg2.l9.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">// Adaptive Refinement</span>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l10" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">10:</span>
                
               Initialize best configuration <math id="alg2.l10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}^{*}\leftarrow\mathcal{C}^{(0)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}^{*}\leftarrow\mathcal{C}^{(0)}</annotation></semantics></math>, best score <math id="alg2.l10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{*}_{\text{hist}}\leftarrow S^{(0)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mtext>hist</mtext><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{*}_{\text{hist}}\leftarrow S^{(0)}</annotation></semantics></math>, training history <math id="alg2.l10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}\leftarrow\{\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}\leftarrow\{\}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l11" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">11:</span>
                
               <span id="alg2.l11.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <math id="alg2.l11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=1</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="alg2.l11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg2.l11.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>



</div>
<div id="alg2.l12" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">12:</span>
                
                Integrate <math id="alg2.l12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math> into RL training pipeline

</div>
<div id="alg2.l13" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">13:</span>
                
                Train agent with <math id="alg2.l13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math>, collect TensorBoard metrics, update <math id="alg2.l13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l14" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">14:</span>
                
                Summarize key metrics into feedback <math id="alg2.l14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l15" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">15:</span>
                
                Obtain performance score <math id="alg2.l15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l16" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">16:</span>
                
                <span id="alg2.l16.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">if</span> <math id="alg2.l16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{(t)}&gt;S^{*}_{\text{hist}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>&gt;</mo><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mtext>hist</mtext><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{(t)}&gt;S^{*}_{\text{hist}}</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg2.l16.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">then</span>



</div>
<div id="alg2.l17" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">17:</span>
                
                 <math id="alg2.l17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{*}_{\text{hist}}\leftarrow S^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mtext>hist</mtext><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{*}_{\text{hist}}\leftarrow S^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="alg2.l17.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}^{*}\leftarrow\mathcal{C}^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}^{*}\leftarrow\mathcal{C}^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l18" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">18:</span>
                
                <span id="alg2.l18.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg2.l18.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">if</span>
</div>
<div id="alg2.l19" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">19:</span>
                
                <span id="alg2.l19.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">if</span> not converged <span id="alg2.l19.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">then</span>



</div>
<div id="alg2.l20" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">20:</span>
                
                 <math id="alg2.l20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}^{(t+1)}\leftarrow F_{\text{LLM}}(P_{\text{refine}}(\epsilon^{(t)},L_{\text{analysis}},\mathcal{C}^{(t)},\mathcal{H}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mtext>LLM</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>refine</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mtext>analysis</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}^{(t+1)}\leftarrow F_{\text{LLM}}(P_{\text{refine}}(\epsilon^{(t)},L_{\text{analysis}},\mathcal{C}^{(t)},\mathcal{H}))</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
