</figure>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="Sx4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Experiments</h2>

<div id="Sx4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We empirically validate Agent<sup id="Sx4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_sup">2</sup> on diverse single-agent and multi-agent RL benchmarks, comparing its performance to original baselines and analyzing the impact of each component.</p>
</div>
<section id="Sx4.SSx1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Experimental Setup</h3>

<section id="Sx4.SSx1.SSSx1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Environments.</h4>

<div id="Sx4.SSx1.SSSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx1.SSSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Single-agent environments include classic MuJoCo continuous control tasks—Ant, Humanoid, Hopper, and Walker. Each task requires an agent to control a simulated robot for stable and efficient locomotion: Ant involves quadruped movement, Humanoid requires balancing a high-dimensional biped, Walker2d targets bipedal walking, and Hopper focuses on single-legged hopping. We also include the more challenging MetaDrive <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. 2022</a>)</cite>, a large-scale autonomous driving simulator where agents must safely navigate diverse and dynamic traffic scenarios using rich sensory observations.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx4.SSx1.SSSx1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx1.SSSx1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Multi-agent environments comprise the Multi-Agent Particle Environment (MPE) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lowe et al. 2017</a>)</cite> with two scenarios: Simple Spread, where agents cooperate to cover all landmarks with minimal collision, and Simple Reference, which combines movement and communication as agents must infer and reach assigned targets. Additionally, we use the StarCraft Multi-Agent Challenge (SMAC) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Samvelyan et al. 2019</a>)</cite>, evaluating on tasks of increasing difficulty: 8m (homogeneous Marines team), 2s3z (heterogeneous Stalker and Zealot units), and 1c3s5z, a large-scale, heterogeneous team requiring advanced coordination and tactical planning.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx4.SSx1.SSSx2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Baselines.</h4>

<div id="Sx4.SSx1.SSSx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx1.SSSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We select widely used reinforcement learning algorithms as baselines to ensure the generality and credibility of our comparisons. For single-agent experiments, we employ PPO, SAC, and TD3 on the MuJoCo environments, and PPO and SAC on MetaDrive. These algorithms are among the most popular and well-established methods in the continuous control domain, and are commonly used as benchmarks in major RL frameworks.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx4.SSx1.SSSx2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx1.SSSx2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For all multi-agent scenarios, we adopt MAPPO, a classic algorithm that has demonstrated strong performance in many benchmarks such as MPE and SMAC. MAPPO is widely recognized for its stability and scalability in cooperative tasks and is supported by mainstream RL codebases.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx4.SSx1.SSSx2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx1.SSSx2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">As baselines, we use the original MDP model of all scenarios with the default hyperparameter settings of all algorithms, as implemented in the Xuance framework<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al. 2023</a>)</cite>. To ensure fair and consistent comparison, we report the cumulative episode reward as the primary evaluation metric for all environments. For StarCraft scenarios, we additionally report the task success rate to better reflect the performance on complex cooperative tasks.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx4.SSx1.SSSx3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Experimental Details.</h4>

<div id="Sx4.SSx1.SSSx3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx1.SSSx3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">All experiments are conducted on a workstation equipped with a 22-core AMD EPYC 7402 CPU, a NVIDIA RTX 4090 GPU, and 108 GB of RAM. Throughout the automated configuration and optimization process, Claude-Sonnet-3.7 is utilized as the underlying LLMs. Notably, Claude-Sonnet-3.7 has demonstrated strong capabilities in problem analysis and code generation, making it a robust choice for our experiments.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx4.SSx1.SSSx3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx1.SSSx3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For each experiment, we set the total environment interaction steps as follows: 1<math id="Sx4.SSx1.SSSx3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> steps for MuJoCo tasks, 400<math id="Sx4.SSx1.SSSx3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> steps for MetaDrive, 1<math id="Sx4.SSx1.SSSx3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> steps for MPE scenarios, and 400<math id="Sx4.SSx1.SSSx3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, 1<math id="Sx4.SSx1.SSSx3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>, or 2<math id="Sx4.SSx1.SSSx3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> steps for the different SMAC tasks.
The automated optimization pipeline uses 5 rounds of MDP generation followed by 5 rounds of algorithm configuration for MuJoCo, MetaDrive, and MPE environments, while 3 rounds of each are used for SMAC tasks.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="Sx4.SSx2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Experimental Results</h3>

<section id="Sx4.SSx2.SSSx1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">MuJoCo.</h4>

<div id="Sx4.SSx2.SSSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx2.SSSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Across a suite of MuJoCo continuous control tasks, Agent<sup id="Sx4.SSx2.SSSx1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_sup">2</sup> consistently achieves higher cumulative rewards and better learning dynamics compared to the baseline. In <span id="Sx4.SSx2.SSSx1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Ant-v4</span>, PPO improves from 3280.51 to 3831.86, TD3 from 3853.84 to 5981.39, and SAC from 3499.76 to 4372.05 (see Fig. <a href="#Sx4.F2.sf1" title="In Figure 2 ‣ MuJoCo. ‣ Experimental Results ‣ Experiments ‣ Agent²: An Agent-Generates-Agent Framework for Reinforcement Learning Automation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2(a)</span></a>). The training curves (see Fig. <a href="#Sx4.F3.sf1" title="In Figure 3 ‣ MuJoCo. ‣ Experimental Results ‣ Experiments ‣ Agent²: An Agent-Generates-Agent Framework for Reinforcement Learning Automation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3(a)</span></a>, <a href="#Sx4.F3.sf2" title="In Figure 3 ‣ MuJoCo. ‣ Experimental Results ‣ Experiments ‣ Agent²: An Agent-Generates-Agent Framework for Reinforcement Learning Automation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3(b)</span></a>, <a href="#Sx4.F3.sf3" title="In Figure 3 ‣ MuJoCo. ‣ Experimental Results ‣ Experiments ‣ Agent²: An Agent-Generates-Agent Framework for Reinforcement Learning Automation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3(c)</span></a>) further show substantial improvements in sample efficiency and final performance, especially for TD3 and SAC.
After the baseline quickly converges, the episode rewards of Agent<sup id="Sx4.SSx2.SSSx1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_sup">2</sup> continue to increase significantly in the later stages of training.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx4.SSx2.SSSx1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx2.SSSx1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In the more challenging <span id="Sx4.SSx2.SSSx1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Humanoid-v4</span> environment, Agent<sup id="Sx4.SSx2.SSSx1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_sup">2</sup> demonstrates exceptional robustness. Notably, the baseline performance of TD3 is extremely poor (354.79) due to an unsuitable default configuration, while Agent<sup id="Sx4.SSx2.SSSx1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_sup">2</sup> dramatically lifts the score to 5425.46. PPO and SAC also show clear improvements, with final scores rising from 917.64 to 1085.85 and from 4682.83 to 6787.94, respectively (see Fig. <a href="#Sx4.F2.sf2" title="In Figure 2 ‣ MuJoCo. ‣ Experimental Results ‣ Experiments ‣ Agent²: An Agent-Generates-Agent Framework for Reinforcement Learning Automation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2(b)</span></a>). The corresponding training curves (see Fig. <a href="#Sx4.F3.sf4" title="In Figure 3 ‣ MuJoCo. ‣ Experimental Results ‣ Experiments ‣ Agent²: An Agent-Generates-Agent Framework for Reinforcement Learning Automation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3(d)</span></a>, <a href="#Sx4.F3.sf5" title="In Figure 3 ‣ MuJoCo. ‣ Experimental Results ‣ Experiments ‣ Agent²: An Agent-Generates-Agent Framework for Reinforcement Learning Automation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3(e)</span></a>, <a href="#Sx4.F3.sf6" title="In Figure 3 ‣ MuJoCo. ‣ Experimental Results ‣ Experiments ‣ Agent²: An Agent-Generates-Agent Framework for Reinforcement Learning Automation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3(f)</span></a>) illustrate: while Agent<sup id="Sx4.SSx2.SSSx1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_sup">2</sup> with PPO shows a modest improvement after the baseline stabilizes, Agent<sup id="Sx4.SSx2.SSSx1.p2.1.5" class="ltx_sup">2</sup> with SAC and TD3 display large further gains after the baseline converges, highlighting the strong late-stage optimization of Agent<sup id="Sx4.SSx2.SSSx1.p2.1.6" class="ltx_sup">2</sup>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx4.SSx2.SSSx1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx2.SSSx1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For <span id="Sx4.SSx2.SSSx1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Hopper-v4</span> and <span id="Sx4.SSx2.SSSx1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Walker2d-v4</span>, Agent<sup id="Sx4.SSx2.SSSx1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_sup">2</sup> also brings observable gains across all algorithms (see Fig. <a href="#Sx4.F2.sf3" title="In Figure 2 ‣ MuJoCo. ‣ Experimental Results ‣ Experiments ‣ Agent²: An Agent-Generates-Agent Framework for Reinforcement Learning Automation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2(c)</span></a>, Fig. <a href="#Sx4.F2.sf4" title="In Figure 2 ‣ MuJoCo. ‣ Experimental Results ‣ Experiments ‣ Agent²: An Agent-Generates-Agent Framework for Reinforcement Learning Automation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2(d)</span></a>).</p>
</div>
