<section id="Sx4.SSx2.SSSx2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">MetaDrive.</h4>

<div id="Sx4.SSx2.SSSx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx2.SSSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">On the MetaDrive autonomous driving benchmark, Agent<sup id="Sx4.SSx2.SSSx2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_sup">2</sup> consistently outperforms the baseline for both PPO and SAC. With automated optimization, the cumulative reward for PPO increases from 245.34 to 277.54, and for SAC from 178.16 to 259.76. As shown in Fig. <a href="#Sx4.F4.sf1" title="In Figure 4 ‣ MPE. ‣ Experimental Results ‣ Experiments ‣ Agent²: An Agent-Generates-Agent Framework for Reinforcement Learning Automation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4(a)</span></a>, Agent<sup id="Sx4.SSx2.SSSx2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_sup">2</sup> achieves clear overall performance gains. The training curves in Fig. <a href="#Sx4.F5.sf1" title="In Figure 5 ‣ MPE. ‣ Experimental Results ‣ Experiments ‣ Agent²: An Agent-Generates-Agent Framework for Reinforcement Learning Automation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5(a)</span></a>, <a href="#Sx4.F5.sf2" title="In Figure 5 ‣ MPE. ‣ Experimental Results ‣ Experiments ‣ Agent²: An Agent-Generates-Agent Framework for Reinforcement Learning Automation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5(b)</span></a> show that, Agent<sup id="Sx4.SSx2.SSSx2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_sup">2</sup> with PPO achieves a moderate increase in the final convergence level, while Agent<sup id="Sx4.SSx2.SSSx2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_sup">2</sup> with SAC substantially accelerates early training progress and rapidly surpasses the baseline from the beginning. These results highlight the effectiveness of automated optimization even in complex, real-world-like single-agent environments.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx4.SSx2.SSSx3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">MPE.</h4>

<div id="Sx4.SSx2.SSSx3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx2.SSSx3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">On MPE cooperative tasks, Agent<sup id="Sx4.SSx2.SSSx3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_sup">2</sup> yields clear improvements in team performance. For <span id="Sx4.SSx2.SSSx3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Simple Spread</span>, the average episode reward improves from -19.73 to -16.31. For <span id="Sx4.SSx2.SSSx3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Simple Reference</span>, the reward rises from -19.61 to -19.00.
The overall performance comparison is shown in Fig. <a href="#Sx4.F4.sf2" title="In Figure 4 ‣ MPE. ‣ Experimental Results ‣ Experiments ‣ Agent²: An Agent-Generates-Agent Framework for Reinforcement Learning Automation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4(b)</span></a>, and the training curves is shown in Fig.<a href="#Sx4.F5.sf3" title="In Figure 5 ‣ MPE. ‣ Experimental Results ‣ Experiments ‣ Agent²: An Agent-Generates-Agent Framework for Reinforcement Learning Automation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5(c)</span></a>, <a href="#Sx4.F5.sf4" title="In Figure 5 ‣ MPE. ‣ Experimental Results ‣ Experiments ‣ Agent²: An Agent-Generates-Agent Framework for Reinforcement Learning Automation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5(d)</span></a>.</p>
</div>
