<section id="Sx4.SSx2.SSSx4" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">SMAC.</h4>

<div id="Sx4.SSx2.SSSx4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx2.SSSx4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">On challenging SMAC scenarios, Agent<sup id="Sx4.SSx2.SSSx4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_sup">2</sup> consistently improves the task win rate over the baseline. In the <span id="Sx4.SSx2.SSSx4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">8m</span> scenario, win rate increases from 0.77 to 0.94; in <span id="Sx4.SSx2.SSSx4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">2s3z</span>, from 0.82 to 0.85; and in the more difficult <span id="Sx4.SSx2.SSSx4.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">1c3s5z</span>, from 0.17 to 0.23. Figure <a href="#Sx4.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ SMAC. ‣ Experimental Results ‣ Experiments ‣ Agent²: An Agent-Generates-Agent Framework for Reinforcement Learning Automation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> provides an overview of the performance gains across all scenarios.
The training curves in Fig.<a href="#Sx4.F7.sf1" title="In Figure 7 ‣ SMAC. ‣ Experimental Results ‣ Experiments ‣ Agent²: An Agent-Generates-Agent Framework for Reinforcement Learning Automation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7(a)</span></a>,<a href="#Sx4.F7.sf2" title="In Figure 7 ‣ SMAC. ‣ Experimental Results ‣ Experiments ‣ Agent²: An Agent-Generates-Agent Framework for Reinforcement Learning Automation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7(b)</span></a>,<a href="#Sx4.F7.sf3" title="In Figure 7 ‣ SMAC. ‣ Experimental Results ‣ Experiments ‣ Agent²: An Agent-Generates-Agent Framework for Reinforcement Learning Automation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7(c)</span></a> show that in the 8m scenario, Agent<sup id="Sx4.SSx2.SSSx4.p1.1.5" class="ltx_sup">2</sup> achieves both faster convergence and substantially higher final episode rewards compared to the baseline. In the more challenging 1s3c5z scenario, Agent<sup id="Sx4.SSx2.SSSx4.p1.1.6" class="ltx_sup">2</sup> initially matches the baseline, but surpasses it in both cumulative rewards and final performance as training progresses. Moreover, the win rate curves in Fig.<a href="#Sx4.F7.sf4" title="In Figure 7 ‣ SMAC. ‣ Experimental Results ‣ Experiments ‣ Agent²: An Agent-Generates-Agent Framework for Reinforcement Learning Automation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7(d)</span></a>,  <a href="#Sx4.F7.sf5" title="In Figure 7 ‣ SMAC. ‣ Experimental Results ‣ Experiments ‣ Agent²: An Agent-Generates-Agent Framework for Reinforcement Learning Automation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7(e)</span></a>,  <a href="#Sx4.F7.sf6" title="In Figure 7 ‣ SMAC. ‣ Experimental Results ‣ Experiments ‣ Agent²: An Agent-Generates-Agent Framework for Reinforcement Learning Automation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7(f)</span></a> reflect similar trends, with Agent<sup id="Sx4.SSx2.SSSx4.p1.1.7" class="ltx_sup">2</sup> achieving higher win rates, especially in complex environments where the improvements are even more pronounced. These results highlight the effectiveness of automated optimization for multi-agent tactical cooperation in challenging scenarios.</p>
</div>
